window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-32"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "Every part of this is the same machine with edges added or removed, so the thing I want to get right in the first minute is the vocabulary: what states the pack can be in at the end of an interval. Every bug in this problem is a state that was needed and not named.", "questions": ["Does a cycle mean a charge and a discharge together, or does each count separately against the warranty?", "Must the pack finish the horizon empty, or is a charge still held at the end worth something?", "Is the settling interval one interval of doing nothing, or one interval in which the pack may still discharge something it holds?"], "assumptions": ["A cycle is a charge and its matching discharge, and it counts against the cap when it opens.", "A plan that finishes still holding a charge has paid for energy it never sold, so the answer comes from a state that leaves the pack empty.", "The settling interval is one interval in which the pack may not charge; it may do nothing else because it holds nothing.", "Prices are non-negative, and a negative price is a broken feed rather than an opportunity."], "edgeCases": ["an empty curve and a curve of one interval, where no trade exists at all", "a strictly falling curve, where doing nothing is the answer and a scheduler without that option reports a loss", "a cap of no cycles, and a cap far larger than the horizon can use", "a discharge in the very last interval, where the settling that follows it never happens"], "restate": "Given a price per interval, schedule charges and discharges of a pack that holds one charge at a time to maximise profit, under four regimes: one cycle, unlimited cycles, at most a given number of cycles, and unlimited cycles with one settling interval after each discharge."}, "solution": {"pitch": "A state machine walked forward once per interval. Two states for the unlimited case, holding and flat, each computed from the previous interval's pair. The cap adds a copy of both per allowed cycle, so the machine gets wider rather than different. The settling rule adds a third state, because whether the pack may charge now depends on something neither existing state records. Naming the states is most of the work; the transitions follow.", "dataStructures": ["Two named scalars for the unlimited case, updated together from the previous interval's values.", "Two arrays indexed by cycle count for the capped case, which is the same pair of scalars with a dimension added.", "A small struct of three named values for the settling case, rebuilt from the previous one each interval rather than mutated in place.", "A named constant standing for a profit no plan can reach, used to seed a state the pack has not entered yet."], "types": ["The three states are a struct with named fields rather than a dictionary keyed on an enum, because the machine is small, fixed, and read on every interval.", "That struct is the domain modelling in this problem. There are no record types on purpose, since adding them would dilute what the problem is actually about.", "The error enum separates a negative cap from a negative price, and the price case carries its interval, because a broken feed is something a caller has to go and find.", "The unreachable seed is a named static constant rather than a literal, so the reason it is half the smallest integer rather than the smallest is visible at the point it matters."], "composition": "The capped scheduler subsumes both simpler ones and the suite proves it rather than describing it: a cap of one reproduces the single-cycle answer on every curve, and a cap the size of the horizon reproduces the unlimited one. That is composition expressed as an invariant, which is the honest form here because the specialised versions genuinely are simpler code and not just calls. There is one literal call as well: once the cap exceeds half the horizon it cannot bind, and the capped scheduler hands the work to the unlimited one.", "tradeoff": "Reporting which intervals the pack actually traded in would need the whole table kept plus a record of each decision, and the rolling scalar form forfeits it. I am keeping the scalar form because the question asks for the profit, and I would rather say what the reconstruction costs than pretend it is free. The other tradeoff is the short circuit on a large cap, which is the optimisation that matters; the storage type of the arrays is not.", "trap": "The seeds. A holding state seeded at nothing earned claims the pack can hold a charge for free, and an unreachable state seeded at the smallest integer overflows the moment a price is added to it, which in Swift ends the process rather than returning a wrong number. The second trap is updating the states in place: it happens to be harmless in the unlimited case and is simply wrong in both parts that follow, which is why the habit is worth more than that part's answer."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the validation first, since all four parts need it and it is four lines.", "Write the single-cycle pass and say out loud that it is the same fold as the largest contiguous run of differences.", "Write the two-state machine next, naming the states in comments before writing either transition.", "Check it on a rising curve and a falling curve before adding any dimension to it.", "Add the cycle count dimension, writing the seeds before the loop because that is where this part goes wrong.", "Add the short circuit for a cap that cannot bind, as a call into the part already written rather than a special case.", "Write the settling machine last, starting by saying why the two-state machine cannot express the constraint."], "commentary": ["I update the cheapest price after asking the question, not before, so the pack never charges and discharges in the same interval.", "There are exactly two states here: holding a charge, or flat. I want them named before I write either transition.", "Both come from the previous interval's pair and I assign them together. Updating one from the other's new value quietly allows a same-interval round trip.", "Holding is seeded at what charging in the first interval costs, not at nothing earned. Nothing earned would mean holding a charge for free.", "Entry t of the holding row is the best the pack can be worth while holding, having opened at most t cycles. That sentence is what the whole loop is doing.", "The seed is half the smallest integer rather than the smallest, because I add a price to it on the very next line and the smallest would trap.", "A cycle needs two intervals, so once the cap reaches half the horizon it cannot bind. That is a call into the part I already wrote, not a special case of this one.", "The two-state machine cannot say whether the previous interval was a discharge, so I am adding a state rather than a condition. Charging is reachable only from flat, and that missing edge is the constraint."], "checkIn": "Before the third part, say: I am going to write the capped version as the general machine and let the first two stand as the special cases they are, rather than deleting them. Does that read as useful, or would you rather I collapsed everything into the general form?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the worked curve of seven, one, five, three, six and four through the single-cycle pass: the cheapest so far falls to one at the second interval and the best discharge is six, giving five.", "Walk the same curve through the two-state machine: one to five and three to six, which is four plus three, and the answer is seven rather than five because the pack may cycle twice.", "Walk a curve with three separate rises: the best single rise is nine, the best two are fifteen, and all three are twenty, which is exactly what a cap of one, a cap of two and no cap must report.", "Walk the settling curve of one, two, three, nought and two: selling into the third interval leaves the fourth settling and the fifth with nothing to sell, giving two, while selling into the second interval leaves the fourth free to charge at nought and the fifth to sell at two, giving three.", "Walk a cap of a billion on a six-interval curve and confirm the short circuit answers it without ever sizing an array to the cap."], "tests": "Run swift test --filter Problem32StorageArbitrageSchedulerTests and read the four suites in part order. The wrong-order fixture in the first suite catches subtracting the minimum from the maximum; the cap-of-one and loose-cap tests in the third suite are the invariants that prove the general machine subsumes the two special cases; the cap-of-two test is the one that catches a machine that only ever handles one or unlimited; and the busy-curve test in the fourth suite is what catches a settling state that is not actually blocking anything.", "complexity": "The first two parts are linear in the number of intervals with a constant number of values carried. The capped part is the number of intervals times the cap, with space linear in the cap, and the short circuit is what keeps an absurd cap from mattering: once the cap exceeds half the horizon the work drops back to linear. The settling part is linear again, with three values instead of two.", "moreTime": "Reporting the intervals the pack actually traded in, which needs the full table plus a decision record and is where the rolling scalar form has to be given up. A per-cycle fee, which is one constant subtracted on exactly one edge of the round trip and is a good test of whether the machine is understood. A settling period of more than one interval, which turns the single settling state into a small queue of them."}, "code": "public enum ScheduleError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case negativeCycleCap\n    case negativePrice(index: Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct ArbitrageScheduler: Sendable {\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - One charge and one discharge\n\n    /// The best profit from charging once and discharging once, in that order.\n    ///\n    /// One pass, carrying the cheapest interval seen so far. Every interval asks\n    /// one question: if I discharge here, having charged at the cheapest price I\n    /// have already seen, what do I make? The answer is the best of those.\n    ///\n    /// This is also the largest contiguous run of the interval-to-interval price\n    /// differences, which is worth noticing out loud: it is the same fold under\n    /// a different name.\n    public func bestProfitSingleCycle(prices: [Int]) throws(ScheduleError) -> Int {\n        try validate(prices: prices)\n        guard let first = prices.first else { return 0 }\n\n        var cheapestSoFar = first\n        var best = 0\n        for price in prices.dropFirst() {\n            best = max(best, price - cheapestSoFar)\n            cheapestSoFar = min(cheapestSoFar, price)\n        }\n        return best\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Unlimited cycles\n\n    /// The best profit with no limit on how many times the pack cycles.\n    ///\n    /// Two states, and naming them is the whole design: at the end of an\n    /// interval the pack is either holding a charge or flat. Both are computed\n    /// from the previous interval's pair and assigned together, never one from\n    /// the other's new value, because updating in place quietly permits charging\n    /// and discharging within the same interval.\n    ///\n    /// The pack starts flat with nothing earned, and charging in the first\n    /// interval costs its price.\n    public func bestProfitUnlimited(prices: [Int]) throws(ScheduleError) -> Int {\n        try validate(prices: prices)\n        guard let first = prices.first else { return 0 }\n\n        var holding = -first\n        var flat = 0\n        for price in prices.dropFirst() {\n            let previousHolding = holding\n            let previousFlat = flat\n            holding = max(previousHolding, previousFlat - price)\n            flat = max(previousFlat, previousHolding + price)\n        }\n        return flat\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - A hard cycle cap\n\n    /// The best profit when the warranty allows at most `cycleCap` full cycles.\n    ///\n    /// The same two states, one copy of each per cycle count. Entry t of the\n    /// holding row is the best the pack can be worth while holding, having\n    /// opened at most t cycles; entry t of the flat row is the same while flat,\n    /// having closed at most t.\n    ///\n    /// A cycle needs two intervals, one to charge and one to discharge, so once\n    /// the cap is at least half the horizon it cannot bind any more. Detecting\n    /// that and handing the work to the unlimited scheduler is what keeps an\n    /// absurd cap from allocating an absurd array, and it is a call into a part\n    /// that is already written rather than a special case of this one.\n    public func bestProfit(prices: [Int], cycleCap: Int) throws(ScheduleError) -> Int {\n        guard cycleCap >= 0 else { throw .negativeCycleCap }\n        try validate(prices: prices)\n        guard !prices.isEmpty, cycleCap > 0 else { return 0 }\n        guard 2 * cycleCap < prices.count else { return try bestProfitUnlimited(prices: prices) }\n\n        var holding = Array(repeating: Self.unreachableProfit, count: cycleCap + 1)\n        var flat = Array(repeating: 0, count: cycleCap + 1)\n        for price in prices {\n            for cycles in 1...cycleCap {\n                holding[cycles] = max(holding[cycles], flat[cycles - 1] - price)\n                flat[cycles] = max(flat[cycles], holding[cycles] + price)\n            }\n        }\n        return flat[cycleCap]\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - A settling interval after each discharge\n\n    /// The best profit when the pack needs one settling interval after every\n    /// discharge before it may charge again.\n    ///\n    /// The two-state machine cannot express this, because \"may I charge now\"\n    /// depends on whether the previous interval was a discharge and neither\n    /// state records that. So the machine gains a state rather than the code\n    /// gaining a special case: discharged-this-interval is now its own state,\n    /// and charging is reachable only from flat.\n    ///\n    /// The answer is the better of the two states that leave the pack empty,\n    /// since a plan that ends holding a charge has paid for energy it never sold.\n    public func bestProfitWithSettling(prices: [Int]) throws(ScheduleError) -> Int {\n        try validate(prices: prices)\n        guard let first = prices.first else { return 0 }\n\n        var state = MachineState(holding: -first, settling: 0, flat: 0)\n        for price in prices.dropFirst() {\n            let previous = state\n            state = MachineState(\n                holding: max(previous.holding, previous.flat - price),\n                settling: previous.holding + price,\n                flat: max(previous.flat, previous.settling)\n            )\n        }\n        return max(state.flat, state.settling)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared machinery\n\n    /// A profit no plan can reach, used to seed a state the pack cannot be in\n    /// yet. Half of the smallest Int rather than the smallest Int, so that\n    /// adding a price to it stays a number instead of trapping.\n    private static let unreachableProfit = Int.min / 2\n\n    /// The three states of the pack at the end of an interval. Written out as a\n    /// type because the state machine is what this problem is about, and naming\n    /// the states is most of the work of getting the transitions right.\n    private struct MachineState {\n        /// Holding a charge bought at some earlier interval.\n        var holding: Int\n        /// Discharged during this interval, so the next one is the settling one.\n        var settling: Int\n        /// Empty and free to charge.\n        var flat: Int\n    }\n\n    /// A price curve has no negative prices. A caller that sends one has a\n    /// broken feed rather than an arbitrage opportunity, and the index is\n    /// reported because that is what makes the feed debuggable.\n    private func validate(prices: [Int]) throws(ScheduleError) {\n        for (index, price) in prices.enumerated() where price < 0 {\n            throw .negativePrice(index: index)\n        }\n    }\n}\n"};
